# Scenario: event timers armed during a request are disarmed again by the time
# it ends, including on the paths where the request does NOT end normally.
#
# WHAT THIS ADDS OVER EVERY OTHER DELTA SCENARIO. conn-delta watches
# connections.free and fds; alloc-per-request watches the three cycle-pool
# counters. A leaked ngx_event_t timer moves NONE of them. The connection is
# closed, its fd is returned, its request pool is freed -- and an entry the
# module armed on that request is still sitting in the worker's event-timer
# rbtree, holding a callback that will fire against freed request state. Every
# oracle this repo shipped before the `timers` field read clean through exactly
# that bug.
#
# WHY THE ABORTED CASES ARE THE POINT. A request that completes normally runs
# the disarm path every request runs, which every other scenario exercises
# thousands of times. The interesting shape is a request that is torn down
# EARLY -- RST mid-header, FIN while the server still owes a response -- because
# a module that disarms its timer from the response path and not from a cleanup
# handler leaks on precisely those and on nothing else. That is the teardown
# path almost nobody tests.
#
# WHY probe_baseline AND NOT ONLY delta. `delta` is case-local (prober.c:327
# fetches the before-snapshot per case), so a leak of exactly one timer per
# request satisfies every per-case `delta timers == 0` while the rbtree grows
# monotonically -- both snapshots already include the drift. `probe_baseline`
# compares against the fixed origin read before case 1, which is the only
# assertion here that can see cumulative growth. This is the same trap
# conn-delta documents for connection slots, and the reason its closing case
# needs an absolute floor.
#
# WHY == 0 IS PORTABLE HERE, UNLIKE cycle_used. The absolute cycle-pool numbers
# depend on build and conf, so alloc-per-request can only assert relative zero.
# The timer count does not: with keepalive off and no request in flight but the
# probe's own, a healthy worker parks at zero armed timers, and the probe
# request itself is answered from the content handler without arming one. That
# was measured, not assumed -- see NON-VACUITY below, where the baseline reads 0
# and a stalled client reads 3.
#
# WHY keepalive_timeout 0 IN THE CONF. A kept-alive connection holds an armed
# timer between requests on purpose. That is not a leak, but it is
# indistinguishable from one at the closing baseline, so the conf takes it off
# the board rather than teaching the assertion to tolerate it.
#
# NON-VACUITY (measured locally against nginx 1.29.0, ref module loaded).
# The gauge distinguishes the states it has to distinguish:
#   - idle worker, no clients            -> "timers":0
#   - three clients stalled mid-header   -> "timers":3   (connections.free 64->58)
#   - the same three gone                -> "timers":0
# A gauge that returned a constant would have read 0 in the middle case, and a
# broken walk would have read 0 everywhere -- which is why the middle reading is
# the one that matters. The mutate.sh row pins the emitter side.

name    baseline before the batch
send    GET /__probe HTTP/1.1\r\n
send    Host: prober\r\nConnection: close\r\n\r\n
expect  status=200
probe   timers == 0
probe   fds >= 1

# A dribbled header block: the read path is re-entered once per chunk and
# client_header_timeout is re-armed on each re-entry, so this case arms and
# disarms a timer several times over. A module re-arming per read without
# disarming leaks here in proportion to the chunk count.
name       dribbled header block completes
send       GET /__probe HTTP/1.1\r\n
send_slow  4 20
send       Host: prober\r\nConnection: close\r\n\r\n
expect     status=200
delta      timers == 0
delta      fds == 0

# A client that vanishes mid-header, with a header timeout armed at the moment
# it does. No `expect`: the connection is destroyed before the server can write
# a response, so the deltas are the assertion.
name    aborted mid-header with a timer armed
send    GET /__probe HTTP/1.1\r\n
send    Host: prober\r\n
abort   24
delta   timers == 0
delta   fds == 0

name    aborted again, immediately after
send    GET /__probe HTTP/1.1\r\n
send    Host: prober\r\n
abort   24
delta   timers == 0
delta   fds == 0

# FIN while the server still owes a response: a graceful EOF where the event
# loop expects one, which unwinds through a different path from a reset.
name     half-closed before the response
send     GET /__probe HTTP/1.1\r\n
send     Host: prober\r\nConnection: close\r\n\r\n
shutdown 1
delta    timers == 0
delta    fds == 0

name     half-closed again, immediately after
send     GET /__probe HTTP/1.1\r\n
send     Host: prober\r\nConnection: close\r\n\r\n
shutdown 1
delta    timers == 0
delta    fds == 0

# The closing case. Its `delta` lines say nothing the earlier ones did not; the
# BASELINE line is what this case exists for. Six requests have run by now, four
# of them torn down early, so a module leaking one timer per aborted request
# sits at four armed timers with every per-case delta above still reading zero.
name    timers returned to baseline after the batch
send    GET /__probe HTTP/1.1\r\n
send    Host: prober\r\nConnection: close\r\n\r\n
expect  status=200
delta   timers == 0
delta   fds == 0
probe_baseline timers == 0
